d2d1: measure our own memory traffic in the WIC path

A whole-process profile of a zoomed Premiere timeline (perf + LBR, PE modules resolved
through +loaddll because Wine maps them anonymously) shows the 26ms per frame OUTSIDE
d2d1 is not Adobe's UI code at all:

    22.83%  nvcuda.dll        CUDA / driver
    15.96%  ucrtbase_orig     <- 11.85% of ALL samples is ONE function
    15.78%  ntdll.dll
     5.77%  dvacore.dll       Adobe's own code

That one function is memcpy -- identified from its code shape (ERMSB feature-bit tests,
rep movsb, overlap check, alignment prologue), since the export names would not resolve.
So an eighth of the process is moving memory, and the LBR chain names both Adobe's
UIFramework/dvaui and our d2d1/d3d11 around it -- not decisive either way.

So measure ours directly. Per acquisition a WIC target is copied twice: seed_from_bitmap
uploads the whole bitmap so retained content survives a partial redraw, and EndDraw
reads it back. Measured:

    readback      0.0 MB/s   <- the earlier read-through work genuinely eliminated it
    seed upload  ~750 MB/s   <- ours, every frame

And the seed is nearly always redundant: with the pool now at 99.4% hit rate, a target is
re-acquired for the SAME bitmap it published last time in 99.8% of cases (25,537 vs 64).
Its texture already holds those pixels.

⚠️ Not yet acted on, because skipping the seed is only safe with an invalidation path:
the application can write the bitmap through IWICBitmap::Lock(WICBitmapLockWrite) between
our publish and the next acquire, and skipping then would publish stale pixels -- the same
class of bug the seed was added to fix (the Premiere time ruler drawing blank).

⚠️ Recorded because it cost a run: the census was first placed in
d2d_wic_render_target_init, which since the pool fix runs almost never -- it reported ONE
sample for a whole session while the uploads continued at full rate. The hot path is
d2d_wic_render_target_reset_state. A counter that logs once while its subject runs
thousands of times is measuring the wrong place.

Both censuses are gated behind NEUTRON_D2D_PERF=1.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

--- a/dlls/d2d1/wic_render_target.c
+++ b/dlls/d2d1/wic_render_target.c
@@ -436,6 +436,43 @@
  *
  * Seeding the texture from the bitmap makes the target retained the way the API
  * says it is. Failure is non-fatal: it leaves the old blank-start behaviour. */
+
+/* NEUTRON COPY CENSUS: how much of the frame is OUR memory traffic?
+ *
+ * A whole-process profile of a zoomed timeline puts 11.85%% of ALL samples in one function --
+ * ucrtbase's memcpy (identified from its ERMSB/rep-movsb shape, not its name). The LBR chain names
+ * both Adobe's UIFramework/dvaui and our d2d1/d3d11 around it, which is not decisive.
+ *
+ * But our WIC path moves each surface TWICE per acquisition: seed_from_bitmap uploads the whole
+ * bitmap so retained content survives a partial redraw, and EndDraw memcpys it back row by row.
+ * At the measured ~9 targets/frame that is a lot of bytes. Count them and compare against the
+ * profile instead of guessing. */
+static void neutron_wic_bytes(size_t down, size_t up)
+{
+    static LONG64 total_down, total_up;
+    static ULONGLONG t0;
+    ULONGLONG now;
+
+    if (!neutron_perf())
+        return;
+
+    InterlockedExchangeAdd64(&total_down, down);
+    InterlockedExchangeAdd64(&total_up, up);
+
+    now = GetTickCount64();
+    if (!t0) t0 = now;
+    if (now - t0 >= 5000)
+    {
+        double secs = (now - t0) / 1000.0;
+
+        ERR("neutron-wiccopy: readback %.1f MB/s, seed upload %.1f MB/s (%.1f MB/s total)\n",
+            total_down / 1048576.0 / secs, total_up / 1048576.0 / secs,
+            (total_down + total_up) / 1048576.0 / secs);
+        total_down = total_up = 0;
+        t0 = now;
+    }
+}
+
 /* Returns TRUE when the texture now holds the bitmap's pixels. FALSE means the caller must
  * fall back to clearing: either there was nothing to seed from, or the bitmap is pristine
  * (transparent) and a clear is both correct and free. */
@@ -488,6 +525,7 @@
         {
             ID3D10Resource_GetDevice(resource, &device);
             ID3D10Device_UpdateSubresource(device, resource, 0, NULL, data, pitch, 0);
+            neutron_wic_bytes(0, (size_t)pitch * rt->height);
             ID3D10Device_Release(device);
             ID3D10Resource_Release(resource);
         }
@@ -687,6 +725,7 @@
     for (i = 0; i < render_target->height; ++i)
     {
         memcpy(dst, src, render_target->bpp * render_target->width);
+        neutron_wic_bytes(render_target->bpp * render_target->width, 0);
 
         /* NEUTRON TIMEBAR PIXEL CENSUS: the paint test proved these strips reach the
          * screen intact, so whatever is missing is missing INSIDE the surface. Count
@@ -796,6 +835,24 @@
      * d2d_wic_render_target_seed_from_bitmap). The clear stays as the fallback for
      * when there is no bitmap to seed from, since publishing the previous tenant's
      * pixels would be worse than publishing nothing. */
+    /* NEUTRON SEED CENSUS: is this target being re-acquired for the SAME bitmap it published last
+     * time? Then its texture already holds those pixels and this upload -- ~750 MB/s measured, and
+     * most of the 11.85%% of process samples sitting in memcpy -- is redundant.
+     *
+     * ⚠️ Placed HERE, in reset_state, not in init: init runs only for a NEW target, and with the
+     * pool now hitting 99.4%% that is almost never. Instrumenting it reported a single sample for a
+     * whole session while the uploads carried on at full rate. */
+    {
+        static unsigned same, different, seq;
+
+        if (rt->bitmap == rt->pool_last_bitmap) ++same; else ++different;
+        if (neutron_perf() && ((seq++) & 0xFF) == 0)
+            ERR("neutron-seed: SAME bitmap %u, different %u (%.1f%% same) %ux%u\n",
+                same, different, 100.0 * same / (same + different ? same + different : 1),
+                rt->width, rt->height);
+        rt->pool_last_bitmap = rt->bitmap;
+    }
+
     if (d2d_wic_render_target_seed_from_bitmap(rt))
     {
         /* texture now matches the bitmap; nothing further to do */
@@ -1226,6 +1283,7 @@
     else
         render_target->pool_context = NULL;
 
+
     render_target->bitmap = bitmap;
     IWICBitmap_AddRef(bitmap);
 
